hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2883","**
Narrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la and ‘Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi (may Allah have mercy upon them), both said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, he informed me: Yunus narrated to me, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, that Salim ibn ‘Abdullah informed him, that ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Umar (RA) used to allow the weak among his family to depart early. Some of them would leave Mina for the Fajr prayer, while others would leave after that. When they arrived, they would stone the Jamrah. Ibn ‘Umar (RA) would say: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) granted concession to these people.""

Abu Bakr (Ibn Abi Shaybah) said: I have recorded in my larger book the various reports of Ibn ‘Abbas regarding this issue, in which the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""O people, do not stone the Jamrah until the sun has risen.""

However, I do not recall a firmly established chain of transmission for these reports from the perspective of narration. If a single chain of transmission is established for any of them, then the meaning is that the Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited those mentioned among them—whom he had allowed to depart early that night—from stoning the Jamrah before the sunrise, not the listener mentioned in the report.

For the report of Ibn ‘Umar (RA) indicates that the Prophet (ﷺ) had indeed granted permission to the weak women to stone the Jamrah before sunrise. Hence, the report of Ibn ‘Umar (RA) does not contradict the report of Ibn ‘Abbas (RA), if the report of Ibn ‘Abbas (RA) is established through a chain of transmission.

In my view, it is also permissible for weak women to stone the Jamrah at night before dawn, based on the report I mention in the chapter that follows, if Allah wills.

---"
